90_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"id":"diagonal-intersection","label":"Establish perpendicular diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-intersection":["cnm_177","cnm_190","cnm_189"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_190","cnm_189","cnm_183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, equality holds if and only if MNPQ is a rectangle","reason":"Triangulating along the two diagonals gives A≤(ab+cd)/2 and A≤(ad+bc)/2. Averaging yields A≤(a+c)(b+d)/4. Equality requires equality in both bounds, forcing all four angles to be right angles; every rectangle attains equality.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midpoint-parallelogram-area-bound","label":"Use the midpoint parallelogram","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-parallelogram-area-bound":["cnm_185","cnm_183","cnm_111"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_183","cnm_111"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5","reason":"FG is parallel and equal to AB. Triangles EFG and HFG have common base FG, and their heights sum to the distance between AB and CD. Their combined area is half of 10, or 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183","cnm_p148","cnm_p147"],"id":"shared-base-height-sum","label":"Split along a shared base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shared-base-height-sum":["cnm_p150","cnm_184"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_p148","cnm_p147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 194","reason":"Since BC=CD, CP is perpendicular to BD. Together with AD⊥BD and AB∥CD, similarity gives AB=2CD=86. The diagonal ratio gives DO=BD/3, so OP=BD/6=11 and BD=66. Thus AD=√(86²-66²)=4√190, giving m+n=194.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"isosceles-parallelogram-similarity","label":"Use an isosceles triangle and similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isosceles-parallelogram-similarity":["cnm_137","cnm_183","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_183","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 15√7","reason":"For face sides a<b<c, coordinate parameters have squares (a²+b²-c²)/8 and its cyclic variants, so a nondegenerate disphenoid exists exactly when the face is acute. Thus a²>c²-b²≥2b+1≥2a+3, giving a≥4. If b≥6, the largest face angle is in [60°,90°), so its area K≥√3ab/4≥6√3>15√7/4. With b=5, only (4,5,6) is acute. Heron gives K=15√7/4, so the attainable total is 15√7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"hinge-acuteness-heron","label":"Unfold the faces and compare areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hinge-acuteness-heron":["cnm_121","cnm_183","math:general-cosine-law-application","math:heron-formula","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_121","cnm_183","math:general-cosine-law-application","math:heron-formula","math:extremal-principle"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 4 inches","reason":"Let the rhombus side be t, with D on AB and E on BC. Since DE is parallel to AC, (12-t)/12=DE/AC=t/6. Solving gives t=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183","cnm_268","cnm_055","cnm_042"],"id":"parallel-side-proportion","label":"Use the parallel sides","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-side-proportion":["cnm_189","cnm_269"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_189","cnm_269","cnm_183","cnm_268","cnm_055","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 20","reason":"The roots are ±(sqrt(10) + i*sqrt(6)) and ±(sqrt(3) + i). The parallelogram has area 2*|sqrt(10) - sqrt(18)| = 6*sqrt(2) - 2*sqrt(10). Thus p + q + r + s = 6 + 2 + 2 + 10 = 20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cartesian-roots-shoelace","label":"Complex roots and coordinate area","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cartesian-roots-shoelace":["cnm_h099","cnm_h102","cnm_h098","math:shoelace-formula","cnm_224","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h099","cnm_h102","cnm_h098","math:shoelace-formula","cnm_224","cnm_183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 80°","reason":"Let H be the midpoint of PG. Then MH is parallel to AG, HN is parallel to PU, and MH = HN = 1/2. Angle MHN is 36° + 56° = 92°, so the base angles are 44°. The requested acute angle is 180° - 56° - 44° = 80°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallelogram-midsegment","label":"Complete a