ch rolling interpretation. The angle itself may be acute or obtuse; both possibilities have the same computed sine."],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183","cnm_p190","cnm_p192","cnm_p206","cnm_h064"],"id":"rhombus-cylinder-dimensions","label":"Identify the circumference and height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rhombus-cylinder-dimensions":["cnm_p207","cnm_276"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p207","cnm_276","cnm_183","cnm_p190","cnm_p192","cnm_p206","cnm_h064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: a+b","reason":"Let X be on AB with DX parallel to BC. Parallelogram XBCD gives XB=CD=b. The parallel-line angle relations and angle D=2 angle B give angle ADX=angle AXD=angle B, so AX=AD=a. Thus AB=a+b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"auxiliary-parallelogram","label":"Split the trapezoid into a triangle and a parallelogram","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-parallelogram":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_184","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 9k/8","reason":"Midpoint similarities give QA=AB=BP, hence QP=3AB. Triangles OQP and OCD are similar with side ratio 3, so O is at 3/4 of the parallelogram height h above QP. Therefore [QPO]=(1/2)(3AB)(3h/4)=9k/8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_270","cnm_069","cnm_063","cnm_055","cnm_183","cnm_p147"],"id":"solution:midpoint-similarities:931ddde2192d","label":"Similar triangles and heights","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-similarities":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_p148","cnm_270","cnm_069","cnm_063","cnm_055","cnm_183","cnm_p147"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_089","cnm_055","cnm_183","cnm_p147"],"id":"solution:coordinate-intersections:931ddde2192d","label":"Coordinates with an arbitrary slant","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-intersections":["cnm_084","cnm_h172","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_h172","cnm_p148","cnm_082","cnm_089","cnm_055","cnm_183","cnm_p147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: √3","reason":"ABEF and BCDE are parallelograms because each has a pair of equal parallel opposite sides. Their adjacent sides all have length 1, and each has an included angle of 60°. Each area is 1·sin60°=√3/2, so the total area is √3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_183","cnm_080"],"id":"split-parallelograms","label":"Split the figure into two parallelograms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"split-parallelograms":["cnm_p150","cnm_184","cnm_p147","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_184","cnm_p147","cnm_174","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_183","cnm_080"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 784","reason":"Write B = (b,b) and D = (d,kd), with b,d positive integers. The area is bd(k - 1), so b·d·(k - 1) = 10^6 = 2^6·5^6. For each prime, its exponent 6 can be distributed among three factors in C(8,2) = 28 ways. These independent choices give 28^2 = 784 distinct parallelograms.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_p147","cnm_174","cnm_083","cnm_183"],"id":"coordinate-area-and-prime-exponents","label":"Convert area into a product of three integers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-area-and-prime-exponents":["cnm_084","math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h230","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h230","cnm_h231","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p147","cnm_174","cnm_083","cnm_183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 75°","reason":"Let F be the perpendicular foot from D to CM. Then DF = AD = 3 and DC = 6, giving angle DCM = 30°. Parallel sides give angle CDM = angle AMD = angle CMD, so twice the requested angle plus 30° equals 180°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_183","cnm_062"],"id":"equal-angles-and-equilateral-reflection","label":"Use equal angles, then construct an equilateral triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-angles-and-equilateral-reflection":["cnm_058","cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_132","cnm_139","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_132","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_p077","cnm_183","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, HE = HG is not necessarily correct","reason":"Midpoint relations give AEHF a parallelogram, BH = CD, G the midpoint of BH, and FG = 3AB/4. For A = (0,0), B = (4,0), C = (0,4), the construction gives HE = 2 but HG = √5, disproving B in general.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q48","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_067","cnm_110","cnm_180"],"id":"solution:midpoint-geometry:bcecd501ec15","label":"Use midpoint geometry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-geometry":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_185","cnm_174","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_184","cnm_183","cnm_185","cnm_174","cnm_071","cnm_067","cnm_110","cnm_180"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 16","reason":"The displayed order F,G,E,B gives GE=32-24=8. Similarity gives AE/CE=FE/BE and AE/CE=BE/GE. Therefore BE²=FE·GE=32·8=256, so BE=16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q38","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_183","cnm_055","cnm_069","cnm_063","cnm_270","cnm_p210","cnm_075"],"id":"two-aa-similarities","label":"Compare two pairs of similar triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"two-aa-similarities":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_220","cnm_183","cnm_055","cnm_069","cnm_063","cnm_270","cnm_p210","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:92bce8f063c02a5ab65fed49"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 4/5","reason":"Extend AB and CD to meet at X beyond A and D. In triangle BDX, the exterior angle BDC gives angle BXD = 46° - 23° = 23°, so XD = BD = 1. Since triangles XAD and XBC are similar, XD/XC = AD/BC = 5/9. Hence CD = XC - XD = 9/5 - 1 = 4/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_183"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p210","cnm_058","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"construct-angle-bisector","label":"C